WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:12.609
This chapter compares four oxygen masks: the partial rebreathing mask, the nonrebreathing mask, the aerosol mask and the air-entrainment, or Venturi, mask.

2
00:00:12.609 --> 00:00:17.766
We start with the deck's dividing line, low-flow versus high-flow.

3
00:00:17.766 --> 00:00:23.078
Then the one difference between the two reservoir masks, the valves.

4
00:00:23.078 --> 00:00:36.125
We finish with how an air-entrainment device sets its FiO2 and total flow, how to calculate that flow, and what it means when the mist disappears from an aerosol mask.

5
00:00:37.533 --> 00:00:46.182
The deck's teaching line: a low-flow device gives a variable FiO2, which depends on the patient's breathing.

6
00:00:46.182 --> 00:00:54.511
A high-flow device gives a fixed FiO2, because its flow meets or exceeds the patient's inspiratory flow.

7
00:00:54.511 --> 00:01:06.122
The deck lists the partial rebreathing and nonrebreathing masks as low-flow, and the Venturi mask and the air-entrainment nebulizer as high-flow.

8
00:01:06.122 --> 00:01:16.933
No deck slide gives FiO2 or flow numbers for the reservoir masks or the aerosol mask, so those numbers come from Hess chapter fourteen.

9
00:01:18.333 --> 00:01:34.242
The partial rebreathing mask delivers an FiO2 of point four zero to point seven zero at eight to fifteen liters per minute, through a three hundred to six hundred milliliter bag with no valves.

10
00:01:34.242 --> 00:01:37.375
Keep the bag at least partly inflated.

11
00:01:37.375 --> 00:01:51.883
The nonrebreathing mask uses the same bag plus one-way valves, between the bag and mask and on the exhalation port, at ten to fifteen liters per minute, often more than twelve.

12
00:01:51.883 --> 00:02:00.950
Hess's text gives an FiO2 of point six to point eight, while its recap says point six zero to point nine zero.

13
00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:09.852
Use point six to point eight, which Hess's own table says is the real range, because the mask does not seal.

14
00:02:09.852 --> 00:02:14.633
Don't let the nonrebreather's bag empty by more than half.

15
00:02:16.033 --> 00:02:24.646
The aerosol mask runs on a large-volume nebulizer, typically with fifteen liters per minute of input.

16
00:02:24.646 --> 00:02:32.918
The deck calls it high-flow, but Hess says it is high-flow only at an FiO2 of point four or less.

17
00:02:32.918 --> 00:02:37.438
Its collar spans about point three to one point zero.

18
00:02:37.438 --> 00:02:50.060
It has no bag; it is an open system on twenty two millimeter corrugated tubing with visible aerosol, and the mist must not disappear on inspiration.

19
00:02:50.060 --> 00:03:01.316
The air-entrainment, or Venturi, mask delivers a precise FiO2 set by its jet or port, but its flow becomes limited above point four.

20
00:03:01.316 --> 00:03:05.239
The minimum jet flow is printed on the device.

21
00:03:05.239 --> 00:03:12.658
Keep the ports uncovered, and aim for a total flow of at least forty liters per minute.

22
00:03:14.067 --> 00:03:22.051
Both reservoir masks are a simple mask with a three hundred to six hundred milliliter reservoir bag.

23
00:03:22.051 --> 00:03:33.549
The partial rebreathing mask has no valves, so the patient breathes a mix of source oxygen, bag oxygen and some room air through the side ports.

24
00:03:33.549 --> 00:03:42.411
The nonrebreathing mask adds one-way valves, between the bag and the mask, and on at least one exhalation port.

25
00:03:42.411 --> 00:03:54.787
On inspiration, the exhalation valves close and the bag valve opens, so the patient draws three hundred to five hundred milliliters of oxygen from the bag.

26
00:03:54.787 --> 00:04:00.217
Removing the valves turns a nonrebreather into a partial rebreather.

27
00:04:01.633 --> 00:04:11.883
Set the flow high enough that the bag never collapses, and for a nonrebreather, so it does not empty by more than half.

28
00:04:11.883 --> 00:04:24.543
Safety matters here: if the valves stick or the oxygen fails, the patient can suffocate, so some masks have a spring-loaded anti-suffocation valve.

29
00:04:24.543 --> 00:04:39.099
Hess uses the nonrebreather for patients who need a high FiO2 with a fairly normal breathing pattern, such as trauma, myocardial infarction and carbon monoxide exposure.

30
00:04:39.099 --> 00:04:44.783
The RCP 105 airway deck uses it to preoxygenate before intubation.

31
00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:47.109
Pause here.

32
00:04:47.109 --> 00:04:52.233
A nonrebreathing mask arrives with its one-way valves missing.

33
00:04:52.233 --> 00:05:01.325
What kind of mask is it now, what does the patient breathe, and why do some masks carry a spring-loaded valve?

34
00:05:02.233 --> 00:05:08.233
[6-second practice pause]

35
00:05:08.733 --> 00:05:13.340
Without its valves, it is a partial rebreathing mask.

36
00:05:13.340 --> 00:05:26.551
The patient breathes a mix of source oxygen, bag oxygen and some room air through the side ports, so the FiO2 is lower, about point four to point seven.

37
00:05:26.551 --> 00:05:40.458
The spring-loaded anti-suffocation valve is there because, on a true nonrebreather, stuck valves or an oxygen failure could leave the patient unable to breathe.

38
00:05:41.867 --> 00:05:52.701
An air-entrainment mask has a jet nozzle that speeds up one hundred percent oxygen, which pulls room air in through side ports.

39
00:05:52.701 --> 00:05:55.602
The deck's rules follow from that.

40
00:05:55.602 --> 00:06:03.365
Bigger ports or smaller jets bring in more air, so the FiO2 falls and the total flow rises.

41
00:06:03.365 --> 00:06:08.911
Smaller ports or bigger jets give a higher FiO2 and a lower flow.

42
00:06:08.911 --> 00:06:16.077
Blocking the ports, with hands or sheets, raises the FiO2 and lowers the total flow.

43
00:06:16.077 --> 00:06:24.267
And changing the oxygen flow in liters per minute does not change the FiO2, only the total flow.

44
00:06:25.667 --> 00:06:32.162
Total flow is the air parts plus the oxygen parts, times the oxygen flow.

45
00:06:32.162 --> 00:06:43.105
Forty percent is a ratio of three to one, so at ten liters per minute of oxygen, four times ten is forty liters per minute.

46
00:06:43.105 --> 00:06:49.956
The same patient on five liters per minute gets four times five, only twenty.

47
00:06:49.956 --> 00:06:57.519
Sixty percent is a ratio of one to one, so two times ten is twenty liters per minute.

48
00:06:57.519 --> 00:07:01.167
Aim for at least forty liters per minute.

49
00:07:02.567 --> 00:07:04.232
Pause and calculate.

50
00:07:04.232 --> 00:07:11.978
A patient is on a sixty percent air-entrainment setting with ten liters per minute of oxygen.

51
00:07:11.978 --> 00:07:16.892
What is the total flow, and does it meet the deck's target?

52
00:07:17.800 --> 00:07:23.800
[6-second practice pause]

53
00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:29.352
Sixty percent is a one to one ratio, two parts in all.

54
00:07:29.352 --> 00:07:38.146
Two times ten is twenty liters per minute, which is below the deck's target of at least forty.

55
00:07:38.146 --> 00:07:47.875
Hess notes the same limit: above an FiO2 of point four, the flow of entrainment devices becomes limited.

56
00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:58.110
The aerosol mask gets its gas from an air-entrainment large-volume nebulizer, which follows the same rules.

57
00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:05.192
If the mist disappears from the mask during inspiration, room air is diluting the gas.

58
00:08:05.192 --> 00:08:11.038
The high-flow system has become a low-flow system with a variable FiO2.

59
00:08:11.038 --> 00:08:18.201
Hess adds that the nebulizer counts as high-flow only at an FiO2 of point four or less.

60
00:08:18.201 --> 00:08:26.600
At point six, its total flow is only thirty liters per minute from fifteen liters per minute of input.

61
00:08:28.000 --> 00:08:30.496
Here is the chapter in brief.

62
00:08:30.496 --> 00:08:39.447
Low-flow devices give a variable FiO2; high-flow devices meet the patient's inspiratory flow and fix it.

63
00:08:39.447 --> 00:08:45.730
The partial rebreather has no valves and gives point four to point seven.

64
00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:56.402
The nonrebreather adds one-way valves and gives about point six to point eight, and its bag must never empty more than half.

65
00:08:56.402 --> 00:09:11.033
An air-entrainment mask sets the FiO2 by its jet and ports; more entrained air means a lower FiO2 and a higher flow, and changing the oxygen liters changes only the flow.

66
00:09:11.033 --> 00:09:18.693
Total flow is the sum of the ratio parts times the oxygen flow, aiming for forty or more.

67
00:09:18.693 --> 00:09:25.750
And if the aerosol mist disappears on inspiration, the system has become low-flow.
